<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project>
  <!--
    Shared C++ build policy for every project in Preview3D.slnx.
    See .docs/design/10-delivery-plan.md ("Repository transition"): C++20,
    Unicode, conformance mode, /permissive-, warning level 4, and
    warnings-as-errors for product code live here instead of drifting
    between per-project .vcxproj files.

    A hostile-worker attack binary (Gate 2's synthetic containment-test
    build) sets IsHostileTestBinary=true in its own .vcxproj to opt out of
    TreatWarningsAsErrors only; it still gets every other policy here.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <WindowsTargetPlatformVersion Condition="'$(WindowsTargetPlatformVersion)'==''">10.0</WindowsTargetPlatformVersion>
    <PlatformToolset Condition="'$(PlatformToolset)'==''">v145</PlatformToolset>
    <CharacterSet Condition="'$(CharacterSet)'==''">Unicode</CharacterSet>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    vcpkg opt-in. A project that consumes a vcpkg dependency declares

      <PropertyGroup Label="Vcpkg">
        <VcpkgEnableManifest>true</VcpkgEnableManifest>
      </PropertyGroup>

    above its Microsoft.Cpp.Default.props import (Preview3DImportWorker,
    Tests.Unit and Tests.ImportIsolation do). Everything else is turned off
    explicitly here rather than left unset.

    Unset is not the same as off: vcpkg defaults VcpkgEnabled to true, finds
    vcpkg.json at the repo root, and prints "The vcpkg manifest was disabled,
    but we found a manifest file in ..." once per project per build. That is
    pure noise for a project that has no vcpkg dependency, and noise in a
    build log is what let a real defect hide in plain sight (see
    .docs/PROGRESS.md, "Release vcpkg DLLs in x64\Debug").

    This changes no build inputs. Those projects already resolved
    _ZVcpkgClassicOrManifest to false, so they were already getting no vcpkg
    include directories, no autolink and no app-local deployment; this only
    stops vcpkg from asking about it. Turning manifests *on* for them would
    not be equivalent: vcpkg autolink appends the whole triplet's lib\*.lib
    to the link line, including the 52 MB basisu_encoder.lib.

    Ordering note: this file is imported (via Microsoft.Common.props, from
    Microsoft.Cpp.Default.props) after a project's own Label="Vcpkg" property
    group is evaluated but before vcpkg.props applies its defaults, so the
    per-project opt-in above still wins.

    Adding a project that needs a vcpkg library? Set VcpkgEnableManifest as
    shown. Forgetting to shows up immediately as an unresolved #include, not
    as a subtle misconfiguration.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <VcpkgEnabled Condition="'$(VcpkgEnabled)'=='' and '$(VcpkgEnableManifest)'!='true'">false</VcpkgEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemDefinitionGroup>
    <ClCompile>
      <LanguageStandard>stdcpp20</LanguageStandard>
      <ConformanceMode>true</ConformanceMode>
      <WarningLevel>Level4</WarningLevel>
      <SDLCheck>true</SDLCheck>
      <AdditionalOptions>/permissive- /utf-8 %(AdditionalOptions)</AdditionalOptions>
      <!-- TreatWarningAsError (not the .NET-SDK-style "TreatWarningsAsErrors"
           property, which has no effect on VC++ ClCompile) is the actual
           /WX switch here. -->
      <TreatWarningAsError Condition="'$(IsHostileTestBinary)'!='true'">true</TreatWarningAsError>
    </ClCompile>
    <Manifest>
      <EnableSegmentHeap>true</EnableSegmentHeap>
    </Manifest>
  </ItemDefinitionGroup>
</Project>
